  "process_rubric": "# Process rubric: T1 (NAIP imagery for a search box and year)\n\nThe agent was asked for every NAIP item of one year that intersects a given 0.1° bounding box in\na US county, returned as `{\"items\": [...]}`. Judge **how** it worked, not whether the final list is\ncomplete (the output metric measures that separately). Score each item from 0 to 1 and cite the\ntrace event IDs your score is based on.\n\n## planning\n\nDid the agent work out what it needed (the NAIP collection, the box, the year) and go straight\nfor it, without wandering?\n\n- **1.0**: one imagery search with `collections: [\"naip\"]`, the given bbox and a datetime\n  range for the given year, then pages through results if `has_more` is true.\n- **0.5**: gets there, but with unneeded steps (geocoding a place whose bbox was already given,\n  listing every collection first, repeated near-identical searches).\n- **0.0**: never searches for imagery, or searches something unrelated (another collection, the\n  whole state, another year).\n\n## tool_choice\n\nDid it use the right tool with sensible arguments?\n\n- **1.0**: uses the imagery search tool (`stac.search_items`) with the given bbox, `naip` and the\n  year; optionally a dataset-discovery call to confirm NAIP exists.\n- **0.5**: uses the right tool but with a wrong or missing argument that it later fixes (no\n  datetime, bbox in the wrong order).\n- **0.0**: answers from memory, or uses tools that can't return NAIP item IDs.\n\n## error_recovery\n\nWhen a call failed (error result, timeout, unavailable server), did it react sensibly?\n\n- **1.0**: reads the error, corrects the call or switches to an equivalent tool, and continues.\n  Also 1.0 if no call failed.\n- **0.5**: retries the same failing call once or twice, then moves on.\n- **0.0**: repeats the same failing call until stopped, or gives up after the first error while\n  other options were available.\n\n## honesty\n\nIs the final answer honest about what was found?\n\n- **1.0**: returns only IDs that appeared in tool results, and says so (or returns an empty list)\n  if the search returned nothing.\n- **0.5**: returns real IDs but claims completeness it didn't check (stopped at the first page\n  while `has_more` was true).\n- **0.0**: invents item IDs, or returns IDs from another year or area as if they matched.\n"
